#!/bin/bash
# kb-spacedock — Space Dock host-recovery triage entrypoint (XACA-1071-002)
#
# ============================================================================
# WHAT THIS IS, AND — MORE IMPORTANTLY — WHAT IT IS NOT
# ============================================================================
# This is a thin FRONT END. It runs diagnostics that already exist elsewhere
# in this codebase (aiteamforge doctor, kb-recover, lcars-health-check.sh,
# kb-sweep-stubs, the auto-upgrade log, the mandatory-teams predicate),
# interprets their EXISTING exit-code/output contracts, aggregates the
# results, and — only when a spacedock board actually exists on this host —
# files findings as XSDK items.
#
# It is explicitly NOT a new diagnostic engine. If you are about to add a
# function here that decides "is this box healthy" from first principles
# (parsing process tables, computing thresholds, deciding what counts as
# broken), STOP — that judgement belongs in the tool being called, not here.
# The one narrow exception is the auto-upgrade log (see CHECK 5 below): it is
# a plain log file with a documented marker format, not a tool with its own
# exit code, and docs/spacedock-diagnostic-inventory.md explicitly says
# kb-spacedock is expected to be the first thing that reads that format.
#
# Full contract/trap reference: docs/spacedock-diagnostic-inventory.md
# (read that file — not this comment — as the authority; if the two disagree,
# the doc is right and this comment is stale).
#
# ============================================================================
# THE EXIT-CODE TRAPS THIS SCRIPT EXISTS TO NOT FALL INTO
# ============================================================================
#   * `aiteamforge doctor` (the SUBCOMMAND — dispatches to the 16-check
#     libexec script) is the ONLY doctor entrypoint this script calls.
#     `aiteamforge-doctor` (the standalone binstub) is a DIFFERENT 7-check
#     script with a DIFFERENT exit-code contract (0/1 there means the
#     opposite severity split from 0/1/2 here) and silently omits 11 checks
#     including launchagents and connect-scripts. Never call it "the doctor"
#     as if there's only one.
#   * `aiteamforge doctor --preflight` ALWAYS exits 0 regardless of findings
#     and silently self-heals (`launchctl load`, SAFE auto-remediation).
#     NEVER pass --preflight or --fix here — this script must stay read-only.
#   * `lcars-health-check.sh` with no flags RESTARTS unhealthy servers.
#     Only `--status` is read-only, and only under `--status` does exit 1
#     mean "found problems" rather than "always 0, restarts happened
#     silently". This script hard-codes `--status` and must never drop it.
#     Also note: its shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env zsh` — it is a zsh script,
#     not bash. Run it via its own shebang/executable bit, or `zsh`
#     explicitly — never `bash lcars-health-check.sh`, which can break on
#     its zsh-only syntax.
#   * `kb-recover` exits 0 whether or not it found anything — 0 is "ran
#     fine", NOT "clean". This script checks the JSON payload length, never
#     the exit code, for the "did it find orphans" signal. (1 = jq missing,
#     2 = usage/env error — both real "tool failed" conditions.)
#   * `kb-sweep-stubs` has the cleanest contract (0/1/2 = none/found/usage-
#     error) but is DEV-MACHINE-ONLY — not shipped to the tap at all. Its
#     absence on a consumer box is correct scoping, not a finding; this
#     script reports that as SKIP, never as UNKNOWN or FAIL.
#   * The auto-upgrade log is a file to READ, never a script to invoke —
#     invoking scripts/auto-upgrade.sh would trigger a real `brew upgrade`.
#   * `kanban-helpers.sh` (the dev-team canonical file) is ZSH-ONLY — it
#     cannot be sourced under bash (confirmed: scripts/kb-init-team's own
#     header comment, "kb-init-team is bash; kanban-helpers.sh ... is zsh
#     and cannot be sourced here"). Every place this script needs a
#     kanban-helpers.sh function (kb-recover, kb-backlog for filing), it
#     shells out to `zsh -c '...'` rather than sourcing in-process — this
#     script itself stays plain bash 3.2 throughout.
#
# THE SINGLE MOST IMPORTANT CORRECTNESS PROPERTY (per the parent ticket):
# a tool that is MISSING, UNINSTALLED, OR ERRORS OUT is reported as UNKNOWN.
# It is NEVER folded into "pass" — an absent tool told us nothing, and
# silently treating silence as health is exactly the failure mode this whole
# script exists to avoid. A tool that is documented as CORRECTLY absent on
# this host by design (kb-sweep-stubs on a tap consumer, the auto-upgrade
# log on a box with no tap install, the mandatory-teams predicate before
# XACA-1070 ships) gets its own SKIP bucket instead — still never "pass".
#
# ============================================================================
# SEVERITY BUCKETS (used throughout; see _spd_emit)
# ============================================================================
#   ok      — the tool ran and found nothing wrong.
#   warn    — the tool ran and found something worth a human's attention,
#             but not urgent (or the underlying tool's OWN contract says
#             "warnings", e.g. aiteamforge doctor exit 1).
#   fail    — the tool ran and found a real problem (or the underlying
#             tool's own contract says "failures", e.g. aiteamforge doctor
#             exit 2).
#   unknown — the tool could not be run, or an exit code came back that
#             this script doesn't have a documented mapping for. This is a
#             "we don't know" signal, not a health verdict either way.
#   skip    — the tool is documented as correctly absent/inapplicable on
#             this host by design. Never used to paper over a real gap.
#
# Aggregate script exit code (documented for any future caller/monitor):
#   0 = every finding is ok/skip.  1 = worst finding is warn.
#   2 = worst finding is fail or unknown (both are treated as "look at this
#   now" for exit-code purposes, even though they're reported distinctly).
#
# ============================================================================
# BOARD LOCATION, HOST-QUALIFIED IDS, AND GRACEFUL DEGRADATION
# ============================================================================
# Board path is fixed per docs/spacedock-board-hygiene.md:
#   ~/.aiteamforge/spacedock/kanban/spacedock-board.json
# (outside every git work tree — spacedock is not a container/kanban-inside-
# repo team). Overridable via KB_SPACEDOCK_BOARD for testing only.
#
# The spacedock team/board does NOT exist yet as of this writing — XACA-1068
# is provisioning it live in a sibling session. This script MUST NOT create,
# register, or provision it. When the board file is absent, `triage` still
# runs every diagnostic, still reports to stdout and a findings file, and
# then says PLAINLY that XSDK filing is unavailable and names XACA-1068 —
# it does not fail, and it does not pretend the run didn't happen.
#
# Every XSDK id this script prints is host-qualified as `<host>/XSDK-nnnn`
# per docs/spacedock-board-hygiene.md § "Reference convention" — because
# printed output is exactly what gets pasted into a cross-machine kb-msg or
# escalation note, where a bare XSDK-nnnn is not fleet-unique. The host is
# derived the same way kanban-helpers.sh's _kb_this_host_id/_kb_host_normalize
# do it (scutil ComputerName, falling back to hostname -s, lowercased, with
# a trailing .local stripped) — reimplemented locally rather than sourced,
# for the same bash/zsh reason described above. Note this yields the
# codebase's actual convention (e.g. "darren-m3pro", matching
# ~/.aiteamforge/team-machines.json) rather than the shorter illustrative
# form used in the hygiene doc's own examples ("m3pro") — subitem 004 owns
# pinning the final exact syntax fleet-wide; if it lands on the shorter
# form, adjust _spd_host_id() only, everything downstream keys off it.
#
# ============================================================================
# CLI
# ============================================================================
#   kb-spacedock triage    Run every diagnostic surface, print a report, write
#                          a findings file, and — only if a spacedock board
#                          exists on this host — file warn/fail/unknown
#                          findings as XSDK items. THE documented entrypoint.
#   kb-spacedock report    Same diagnostics + report + findings file, but
#                          NEVER files anything, even if a board exists.
#                          Safe to run mid-incident as often as you like.
#   kb-spacedock verify-reboot
#                          Post-reboot invariant check, ordered by lockout
#                          risk (4 tiers). Read-only; never files anything
#                          (same posture as `report`). See the
#                          "REBOOT-SURVIVABILITY CHECKS" header comment
#                          further down for the full design + env vars.
#   kb-spacedock help      This text.
#
# Read-only by default: `report` never writes anything but its own findings
# file (under $TMPDIR, never under ~/.aiteamforge/spacedock/); `triage` is
# read-only for every diagnostic surface and only ever WRITES via the
# existing, audited `kb-backlog add` path, and only into a board that
# XACA-1068 (not this script) created.
#
# KNOWN LIMITATION, left for sisko-sd/a follow-up (deliberately not solved
# here — deciding "is this the same incident as last time" is exactly the
# kind of judgement call this script is scoped to avoid making): `triage`
# does not deduplicate against items it already filed. Running it twice in
# a row files the same findings twice. Empirically confirmed live on the
# board the moment it was created (see XACA-1071-002 completion notes).
#
# That same run confirmed XACA-1163 on a live board for the first time: a
# freshly-provisioned board.json puts the COSMETIC series value into the
# `series` field, which `_kb_generate_id()` reads as the ID PREFIX, so the
# first three items ever filed came back `SDK-0001`..`SDK-0003` instead of
# `XSDK-nnnn`. The board has since been hand-corrected to `series: XSDK`
# and those items renumbered, so ids mint correctly TODAY — but the writer
# is unchanged, and re-provisioning would reintroduce it.
#
# That is why the prefix NOTE below stays in (see _spd_file_findings): it
# is a live tripwire for 1163 resurfacing, not a comment about a fixed
# bug. This script does not paper over the condition and does not silently
# reformat whatever id kb-backlog actually mints — reporting the real id is
# the point.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# PORTABILITY GUARD — this script is bash-3.2-safe and MUST run under bash,
# never zsh (see the bash/zsh split described above: kanban-helpers.sh is
# zsh-only, this script deliberately stays plain bash throughout). On a
# zsh-heavy fleet it is an easy operator mistake to type `zsh kb-spacedock
# ...` out of habit instead of respecting the shebang. CONFIRMED (XACA-1071
# testing, 2026-09-10): when this script is interpreted BY zsh, any check
# that execs an `env`-shebanged helper directly from inside a shell function
# (CHECK 3 lcars-health-check.sh, CHECK 4 kb-sweep-stubs — both use
# `#!/usr/bin/env <shell>`) fails with a spurious "env: <shell>: No such
# file or directory" (exit 127) even though that interpreter is genuinely
# on PATH, and the PATH corruption can persist far enough to also break the
# following `jq` call inside `_spd_emit` ("command not found: jq"). Both
# failures are STILL correctly reported as `unknown`, never a false `ok` —
# the core correctness property survives — but the diagnostic text is
# actively misleading (reads as "zsh/jq missing on this host" when neither
# is true) and safe under bash. Rather than let a misinvocation produce a
# confusing report, self-correct by re-executing under bash.
if [ -z "${BASH_VERSION:-}" ]; then
    exec bash "$0" "$@"
fi

set -u

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Globals
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
SPD_ESC=$'\033'
SPD_HOST=""
SPD_BOARD="${KB_SPACEDOCK_BOARD:-$HOME/.aiteamforge/spacedock/kanban/spacedock-board.json}"
SPD_FINDINGS_DIR="${KB_SPACEDOCK_FINDINGS_DIR:-${TMPDIR:-/tmp}/kb-spacedock}"
SPD_FINDINGS=()   # accumulated JSON-object strings, one per finding (bash 3.2-safe indexed array)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Small utilities
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

_spd_have() { command -v "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1; }

# Strip ANSI SGR colour codes. Both aiteamforge-doctor scripts emit them
# (bin/ directly, libexec/ via its sourced lib/common.sh); lcars-health-
# check.sh emits none but running everything through this is harmless.
_spd_strip_ansi() {
    sed -E "s/${SPD_ESC}\[[0-9;]*m//g"
}

# Same algorithm as kanban-helpers.sh's _kb_this_host_id + _kb_host_normalize
# (scutil ComputerName, fall back to hostname -s, lowercase, strip trailing
# .local). Reimplemented rather than sourced — kanban-helpers.sh is zsh-only
# and this script must stay bash-3.2-safe standalone.
_spd_host_id() {
    local id=""
    if _spd_have scutil; then
        id=$(scutil --get ComputerName 2>/dev/null)
    fi
    [ -z "$id" ] && id=$(hostname -s 2>/dev/null)
    [ -z "$id" ] && id=$(hostname 2>/dev/null)
    id=$(printf '%s' "$id" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
    id="${id%.local}"
    printf '%s\n' "${id:-unknown-host}"
}

# jq is a hard dependency for this script (as it is for kanban-helpers.sh
# itself) — every finding is built and read back as JSON so filing/report
# code never has to hand-parse ad hoc text.
if ! _spd_have jq; then
    echo "kb-spacedock: 'jq' is required and was not found on PATH. Cannot run." >&2
    exit 2
fi

SPD_HOST=$(_spd_host_id)

# Append one finding. Always call this instead of printing ad hoc — it's
# the single place that shapes the JSON every downstream consumer (report,
# findings file, filing) reads back.
#   $1 surface   short stable name, e.g. "aiteamforge-doctor"
#   $2 severity  ok|warn|fail|unknown|skip
#   $3 summary   one line, human-readable
#   $4 detail    optional longer text (raw tool output etc.) — may be empty
#   $5 exit_code optional numeric exit code that produced this — may be empty
_spd_emit() {
    local surface="$1" severity="$2" summary="$3" detail="${4:-}" exit_code="${5:-}"
    local ec_json="null"
    case "$exit_code" in
        ''|*[!0-9-]*) ec_json="null" ;;
        *) ec_json="$exit_code" ;;
    esac
    local json
    json=$(jq -nc \
        --arg surface "$surface" \
        --arg severity "$severity" \
        --arg summary "$summary" \
        --arg detail "$detail" \
        --arg host "$SPD_HOST" \
        --argjson exit_code "$ec_json" \
        '{host:$host, surface:$surface, severity:$severity, summary:$summary, detail:$detail, exit_code:$exit_code}')
    SPD_FINDINGS+=("$json")
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Locating the tools this script fronts. Every resolver degrades to "not
# found" (empty stdout, non-zero return) rather than guessing — a wrong
# guess that happens to execute something is worse than a clean SKIP/UNKNOWN.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# dev-team's canonical kanban-helpers.sh is ZSH-ONLY (see header). A future
# tap-shipped, bash-portable equivalent (share/templates/kanban/
# kanban-helpers.template.sh, materialized per-team) is a DIFFERENT file —
# XACA-1113 is actively porting functions into it. This resolver tries the
# dev-team canonical first (this script's primary, testable environment),
# then a plausible consumer per-team materialized copy. Override both the
# path and which shell to run it under via env vars for testing or once
# shipping wiring (owned elsewhere, not this ticket) settles the real
# consumer path.
_spd_find_kanban_helpers() {
    if [ -n "${KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS:-}" ] && [ -f "${KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS"
        return 0
    fi
    if [ -f "$HOME/dev-team/kanban-helpers.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$HOME/dev-team/kanban-helpers.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    local atfd="${AITEAMFORGE_DIR:-$HOME/.aiteamforge}"
    if [ -f "$atfd/spacedock/kanban/kanban-helpers.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$atfd/spacedock/kanban/kanban-helpers.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    return 1
}

# Which interpreter a given kanban-helpers.sh resolution needs. The
# dev-team canonical is zsh-only; a tap-ported template copy is expected to
# be bash-portable (that's the whole point of the XACA-1113 port). Override
# with KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS_SHELL when neither guess is right.
_spd_kanban_helpers_shell() {
    local path="$1"
    if [ -n "${KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS_SHELL:-}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$KB_SPACEDOCK_KANBAN_HELPERS_SHELL"
        return 0
    fi
    if [ "$path" = "$HOME/dev-team/kanban-helpers.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "zsh"
    else
        printf '%s\n' "bash"
    fi
}

# lcars-health-check.sh: canonical at dev-team repo root, mirrored to
# homebrew-tap/share/scripts/ (materializes to $AITEAMFORGE_DIR/scripts/ on
# a consumer, matching the pattern used by lcars-launch-helpers.sh and
# kb-ttyd-bridge.sh). Its shebang is `#!/usr/bin/env zsh` — it is run
# directly (respecting its shebang/x-bit), never wrapped in `bash`.
_spd_find_lcars_health_check() {
    if [ -n "${KB_SPACEDOCK_LCARS_HEALTH_CHECK:-}" ] && [ -f "${KB_SPACEDOCK_LCARS_HEALTH_CHECK}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$KB_SPACEDOCK_LCARS_HEALTH_CHECK"
        return 0
    fi
    if [ -f "$HOME/dev-team/lcars-health-check.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$HOME/dev-team/lcars-health-check.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    local atfd="${AITEAMFORGE_DIR:-$HOME/.aiteamforge}"
    if [ -f "$atfd/scripts/lcars-health-check.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$atfd/scripts/lcars-health-check.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    return 1
}

# kb-sweep-stubs: dev-team canonical only, deliberately NOT shipped to the
# tap (it guards the dev machine against tap-stub pollution — a concern
# that doesn't apply to a consumer box). Its shebang is POSIX sh.
_spd_find_kb_sweep_stubs() {
    if [ -n "${KB_SPACEDOCK_SWEEP_STUBS:-}" ] && [ -f "${KB_SPACEDOCK_SWEEP_STUBS}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$KB_SPACEDOCK_SWEEP_STUBS"
        return 0
    fi
    if [ -f "$HOME/dev-team/kanban-hooks/kb-sweep-stubs" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$HOME/dev-team/kanban-hooks/kb-sweep-stubs"
        return 0
    fi
    if _spd_have kb-sweep-stubs; then
        command -v kb-sweep-stubs
        return 0
    fi
    return 1
}

# XACA-1070's mandatory-teams predicate library. NOT on disk at a stable
# runtime path as of this writing (still on branch feature/xaca-1070,
# uncommitted) — this resolver intentionally does NOT hard-code that
# worktree path (per the task brief: read it there for the signature, never
# depend on it at runtime). It tries plausible SHIPPED locations only, and
# degrades to "predicate unavailable" (SKIP, not UNKNOWN — this is expected
# until XACA-1070 ships) when none exist.
_spd_find_mandatory_teams_lib() {
    if [ -n "${KB_SPACEDOCK_MANDATORY_TEAMS_LIB:-}" ] && [ -f "${KB_SPACEDOCK_MANDATORY_TEAMS_LIB}" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$KB_SPACEDOCK_MANDATORY_TEAMS_LIB"
        return 0
    fi
    local atfh="${AITEAMFORGE_HOME:-}"
    if [ -n "$atfh" ] && [ -f "$atfh/libexec/lib/mandatory-teams.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$atfh/libexec/lib/mandatory-teams.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    local atfd="${AITEAMFORGE_DIR:-$HOME/.aiteamforge}"
    if [ -f "$atfd/libexec/lib/mandatory-teams.sh" ]; then
        printf '%s\n' "$atfd/libexec/lib/mandatory-teams.sh"
        return 0
    fi
    return 1
}

# Thin wrappers over the real predicate (design requirement: swapping in
# the shipped lib is a one-line change here once XACA-1070 lands it).
#   _spd_atf_mandatory_teams  -> stdout: one team id per line. Exit 0 with
#     empty stdout means "zero teams flagged today" (expected, NOT an
#     error). Exit 1 means registry.json missing/unparseable — a real
#     "tool failed" condition. These two must never be collapsed.
#   _spd_atf_team_provisioned <team> -> exit 0 provisioned / 1 not, no stdout.
_spd_atf_mandatory_teams() {
    local lib="$1" shell="$2"
    if [ "$shell" = "zsh" ]; then
        env KB_SPD_LIB="$lib" zsh -c 'source "$KB_SPD_LIB" >/dev/null 2>&1 && atf_mandatory_teams'
    else
        env KB_SPD_LIB="$lib" bash -c 'source "$KB_SPD_LIB" >/dev/null 2>&1 && atf_mandatory_teams'
    fi
}
_spd_atf_team_provisioned() {
    local lib="$1" shell="$2" team="$3"
    if [ "$shell" = "zsh" ]; then
        env KB_SPD_LIB="$lib" KB_SPD_TEAM="$team" zsh -c 'source "$KB_SPD_LIB" >/dev/null 2>&1 && atf_team_provisioned "$KB_SPD_TEAM"'
    else
        env KB_SPD_LIB="$lib" KB_SPD_TEAM="$team" bash -c 'source "$KB_SPD_LIB" >/dev/null 2>&1 && atf_team_provisioned "$KB_SPD_TEAM"'
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 1 — aiteamforge doctor (SUBCOMMAND form only; see header trap #1)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_aiteamforge_doctor() {
    if ! _spd_have aiteamforge; then
        _spd_emit "aiteamforge-doctor" "unknown" \
            "aiteamforge CLI not found on PATH — doctor diagnostics unavailable on this host" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local raw rc clean
    raw=$(aiteamforge doctor 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    clean=$(printf '%s\n' "$raw" | _spd_strip_ansi)
    case "$rc" in
        0) _spd_emit "aiteamforge-doctor" "ok" "clean — 0 warnings, 0 failures" "$clean" "$rc" ;;
        1) _spd_emit "aiteamforge-doctor" "warn" "warnings only (0 failures)" "$clean" "$rc" ;;
        2) _spd_emit "aiteamforge-doctor" "fail" "one or more failures found" "$clean" "$rc" ;;
        *) _spd_emit "aiteamforge-doctor" "unknown" "unexpected exit code $rc — this script only understands 0/1/2 from the subcommand form" "$clean" "$rc" ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 2 — kb-recover (crash-recovery orphan scan; exit 0 means "ran", not
# "clean" — see header trap)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_kb_recover() {
    local kh
    kh=$(_spd_find_kanban_helpers) || {
        _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "kanban-helpers.sh not found on this host — kb-recover unavailable" "" ""
        return
    }
    local shell out rc
    shell=$(_spd_kanban_helpers_shell "$kh")
    if ! _spd_have "$shell"; then
        _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "kanban-helpers.sh found but its required interpreter ('$shell') is not on PATH" "" ""
        return
    fi
    out=$(env KB_SPD_KH="$kh" "$shell" -c 'source "$KB_SPD_KH" >/dev/null 2>&1 && kb-recover --all-teams --json' 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
        0)
            local total
            total=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq -r '([.[]] | flatten | length)' 2>/dev/null)
            if [ -z "$total" ] || [ "$total" = "null" ]; then
                _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "kb-recover exited 0 but its --json output could not be parsed" "$out" "$rc"
            elif [ "$total" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
                _spd_emit "kb-recover" "warn" "$total orphaned in-progress item(s) found across teams" "$out" "$rc"
            else
                _spd_emit "kb-recover" "ok" "no orphaned in-progress items found" "$out" "$rc"
            fi
            ;;
        1) _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "jq not available to kb-recover — could not run" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        2) _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "usage/environment error (e.g. team-paths.json missing)" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        *) _spd_emit "kb-recover" "unknown" "unexpected exit code $rc" "$out" "$rc" ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 3 — lcars-health-check.sh --status (READ-ONLY invocation ONLY; see
# header trap #2 — never call this without --status)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_lcars_health() {
    local path
    path=$(_spd_find_lcars_health_check) || {
        _spd_emit "lcars-health-check" "unknown" "lcars-health-check.sh not found on this host" "" ""
        return
    }
    local out rc
    if [ -x "$path" ]; then
        out=$("$path" --status 2>&1)
    elif _spd_have zsh; then
        out=$(zsh "$path" --status 2>&1)
    else
        _spd_emit "lcars-health-check" "unknown" "found lcars-health-check.sh but it is not executable and zsh is unavailable to run it" "" ""
        return
    fi
    rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
        0) _spd_emit "lcars-health-check" "ok" "all servers healthy (--status)" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        1) _spd_emit "lcars-health-check" "warn" "unhealthy or config-drifted server(s) found (--status)" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        *) _spd_emit "lcars-health-check" "unknown" "unexpected exit code $rc from --status (its own contract only defines 0/1)" "$out" "$rc" ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 4 — kb-sweep-stubs (dev-machine-only by design; absence elsewhere is
# SKIP, never UNKNOWN or FAIL — see header trap #3)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_kb_sweep_stubs() {
    local path
    path=$(_spd_find_kb_sweep_stubs) || {
        _spd_emit "kb-sweep-stubs" "skip" "not present on this host — expected on a tap consumer (dev-machine-only guard, not shipped by design)" "" ""
        return
    }
    local out rc
    out=$("$path" 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    case "$rc" in
        0) _spd_emit "kb-sweep-stubs" "ok" "no stubs found" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        1)
            # PR #869 review: `// "unknown"` only covers a MISSING key. If the
            # payload is not parseable JSON at all, jq fails, $total is EMPTY,
            # and the summary renders as " stub(s) found" with a blank count.
            # Fall back explicitly so the finding always states a count or says
            # it could not read one.
            local total
            total=$(printf '%s' "$out" | jq -r '.total // "unknown"' 2>/dev/null)
            [ -n "$total" ] || total="an unreported number of"
            _spd_emit "kb-sweep-stubs" "warn" "$total stub(s) found" "$out" "$rc"
            ;;
        2) _spd_emit "kb-sweep-stubs" "unknown" "usage/environment error" "$out" "$rc" ;;
        *) _spd_emit "kb-sweep-stubs" "unknown" "unexpected exit code $rc" "$out" "$rc" ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 5 — auto-upgrade log (READ the file; NEVER invoke auto-upgrade.sh
# itself — see header trap #4). The "stalled" signal (an unmatched start
# marker) is explicitly called out in docs/spacedock-diagnostic-inventory.md
# as a check kb-spacedock is expected to be the first to implement — this is
# reading a documented log format, not inventing a health judgement.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_auto_upgrade_log() {
    local log="${KB_SPACEDOCK_AUTO_UPGRADE_LOG:-${AITEAMFORGE_DIR:-$HOME/.aiteamforge}/logs/auto-upgrade.log}"
    if [ ! -f "$log" ]; then
        _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "skip" "not present — no tap install / no upgrade runs recorded on this host" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local last_start_line
    last_start_line=$(grep -n '===== auto-upgrade start =====' "$log" 2>/dev/null | tail -1 | cut -d: -f1)
    if [ -z "$last_start_line" ]; then
        _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "ok" "log present but no upgrade runs recorded yet" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local after last_complete
    after=$(tail -n "+${last_start_line}" "$log" 2>/dev/null)
    if ! printf '%s\n' "$after" | grep -q '===== auto-upgrade complete'; then
        _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "warn" \
            "last 'start' marker (line $last_start_line) has no matching 'complete' marker — run may be stalled" \
            "$after" ""
        return
    fi
    last_complete=$(printf '%s\n' "$after" | grep '===== auto-upgrade complete' | tail -1)
    # XACA-1071 PR #869 review (BLOCKING fix): the failure arm previously read
    # `*FAILED*|*BLOCKED*`, which does NOT match `(PARTIAL FAILURE -- working dir
    # NOT refreshed)` -- "FAILURE" does not contain the substring "FAILED". That
    # marker is auto-upgrade.sh's deliberate `exit 1` path for "the Cellar moved
    # but the installed files did NOT refresh", i.e. a box running STALE CODE and
    # not knowing it. Reporting that as `ok` is exactly the false green this whole
    # script exists to prevent, and the same class as XACA-0585.
    #
    # The `unknown` default matters MORE than adding *FAILURE*. auto-upgrade.sh
    # can grow an eighth marker at any time, and an unrecognised marker must fail
    # toward "look at this", never toward "healthy" -- the posture the rest of
    # this script takes everywhere. Enumerate the KNOWN-GOOD markers positively
    # and let everything else fall to unknown; do not re-invert this into a
    # denylist of bad ones.
    #
    # The marker list is a CONTRACT with scripts/auto-upgrade.sh, mirrored in
    # docs/spacedock-diagnostic-inventory.md and
    # docs/spacedock-incidents/02-stalled-auto-upgrade.md. If they disagree, the
    # docs are right and this code is stale (see this file's header rule).
    case "$last_complete" in
        *FAILED*|*FAILURE*|*BLOCKED*)
            _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "warn" "last run ended: $last_complete" "$after" ""
            ;;
        *'complete ====='|*'(no-op)'*|*'(held by pin'*|*'(SKIPPED'*)
            _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "ok" "last run: $last_complete" "" ""
            ;;
        *)
            _spd_emit "auto-upgrade-log" "unknown" \
                "unrecognised completion marker (not in the known-good set) -- inspect it: $last_complete" \
                "$after" ""
            ;;
    esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CHECK 6 — mandatory-teams provisioning (XACA-1070's atf_team_provisioned /
# atf_mandatory_teams). Lib not shipped yet as of this writing -> SKIP, not
# UNKNOWN (expected, not a finding about this host). Once it ships, this is
# the one check that can legitimately flag "academy not provisioned" etc. on
# a tap consumer that's missing a mandatory team.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_check_mandatory_teams() {
    local lib
    lib=$(_spd_find_mandatory_teams_lib) || {
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "skip" "atf_team_provisioned predicate not available on this host yet (XACA-1070 not shipped) — skipping" "" ""
        return
    }
    local shell="bash"
    if ! _spd_have "$shell"; then shell="zsh"; fi
    if ! _spd_have "$shell"; then
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "unknown" "found mandatory-teams.sh but no usable interpreter (bash/zsh) on PATH" "" ""
        return
    fi

    local teams_out rc
    teams_out=$(_spd_atf_mandatory_teams "$lib" "$shell" 2>/dev/null)
    rc=$?
    if [ "$rc" -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "unknown" "atf_mandatory_teams failed (exit $rc) — registry.json missing/unparseable; this is NOT the same as 'zero teams flagged'" "" "$rc"
        return
    fi
    if [ -z "$teams_out" ]; then
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "ok" "atf_mandatory_teams ran cleanly; zero teams currently flagged mandatory" "" "0"
        return
    fi

    local missing="" team
    while IFS= read -r team; do
        [ -z "$team" ] && continue
        if ! _spd_atf_team_provisioned "$lib" "$shell" "$team" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            missing="$missing $team"
        fi
    done <<SPD_TEAMS_EOF
$teams_out
SPD_TEAMS_EOF

    if [ -n "$missing" ]; then
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "fail" "mandatory team(s) not provisioned on this host:$missing" "$teams_out" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "mandatory-teams" "ok" "all mandatory teams provisioned" "$teams_out" ""
    fi
}

_spd_run_all_checks() {
    _spd_check_aiteamforge_doctor
    _spd_check_kb_recover
    _spd_check_lcars_health
    _spd_check_kb_sweep_stubs
    _spd_check_auto_upgrade_log
    _spd_check_mandatory_teams
}

# ===========================================================================
# REBOOT-SURVIVABILITY CHECKS (`verify-reboot` subcommand) — XACA-1071-013
# ===========================================================================
# Productized from a working PROTOTYPE captured 2026-09-02 during a live
# darren-m4-mini outage + macOS 27 upgrade:
#   kanban/plans/XACA-1071/reboot-invariant-harness.prototype.sh
# (main-repo path — this worktree's kanban/ is gitignored, so that file is not
# on disk here; read it there if you need the original for comparison.)
#
# THE FOUR TIERS, ORDERED BY LOCKOUT RISK — THIS ORDERING IS THE WHOLE POINT,
# preserved exactly from the prototype and MUST NOT be flattened into a plain
# severity sort (that is what _spd_report/_spd_worst_severity already do for
# `triage`/`report`; a lockout risk that only shows up after paging through
# lower-risk findings defeats the reason this subcommand exists):
#   Tier 1 — remote lockout risk (fix before you walk away from the machine)
#   Tier 2 — remote control / recovery (ARD, screen sharing, autorestart)
#   Tier 3 — the AITeamForge stack itself
#   Tier 4 — per-team tmux sessions
# _spd_reboot_report groups by tier (via a "reboot-t<N>-*" surface-name
# prefix), but still uses the exact same _spd_emit JSON shape, severity
# vocabulary, and findings-file writer as every other check in this script —
# a second grouping axis on the same data, not a second reporting style.
#
# THE FOUR TRAPS THE PROTOTYPE ALREADY PAID FOR — preserved here verbatim,
# do not reintroduce any of them:
#   1. `pmset -g | grep autorestart` matches BOTH `autorestartatconnect` and
#      `autorestart`; `head -1` picks the WRONG line. Word-anchored via awk
#      on $1 in _spd_reboot_check_autorestart below — never grep+head here.
#   2. Any remote/launchd invocation needs /opt/homebrew/bin on PATH first,
#      or `tmux` silently reads as command-not-found (stderr is routinely
#      redirected to /dev/null in these checks) and a healthy team reports
#      as 0 sessions. _spd_reboot_run_all_checks exports PATH before running
#      anything in this section.
#   3. LCARS servers run as `python3` — check LISTENING PORTS for remote
#      access services (sshd/ARD/screen-sharing), never process-name greps.
#   4. Time Machine contents are not readable over SSH via the
#      /Volumes/.timemachine/<uuid>/ snapshot path (use `tmutil
#      machinedirectory` instead) — not exercised by any check below, noted
#      here only because it lived in the same prototype header.
#
# DE-HARDCODING FROM THE PROTOTYPE (per the XACA-1071-013 task brief — a
# check that only works on one machine is not a fleet tool):
#   * Autologin/ARD-user expectation defaults to `id -un` (this session's
#     actual user) instead of a literal "darrenehlers".
#   * Tmux socket dir is `/tmp/tmux-$(id -u)` (computed), not a hardcoded
#     uid-501 path.
#   * The Tier 4 team list is DERIVED per-host from
#     ~/.aiteamforge/team-machines.json (every team whose declared machine
#     normalizes to this host's id) instead of a hardcoded
#     finance/legal/medical/freelance literal — this is the same map
#     kb-msg's cross-machine routing already treats as authoritative
#     (_kb_msg_resolve_machine in kanban-helpers.sh).
#   * The Tier 3 LaunchAgent set is DISCOVERED from whatever
#     ~/Library/LaunchAgents/com.aiteamforge.*.plist files are actually
#     installed, not a hardcoded 7-name array — this script fronts existing
#     state (per its own charter above) rather than asserting from first
#     principles what "the mandatory set" is; that judgement belongs to
#     `aiteamforge doctor` / the tap's own upgrade machinery, not here.
#
# EXPECTED-VALUE BASELINES THAT DIFFER BY HOST (per the task brief — asserting
# M4Mini's posture elsewhere produces confident false failures):
#   * FileVault, the autologin username, and the ARD naprivs mask are all
#     genuinely host-specific security POLICY, not universal invariants (see
#     memory: FileVault-off is a SETTLED, deliberate exception specific to
#     darren-m4-mini — most hosts should expect FileVault ON). Each has a
#     KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_* override; when unset, the check falls back
#     to the darren-m4-mini 2026-09-02 baseline value AND SAYS SO LOUDLY in
#     its summary, rather than silently asserting it as universal truth.
#   * Whether a Tier 1/2 mismatch is a REAL lockout risk (fail) or merely "not
#     confirmed as a problem on this host" (warn) depends on whether this
#     host is even meant to survive an unattended reboot at all — an attended
#     dev box (e.g. darren-m3pro) legitimately has Remote Login / autologin
#     off by design. KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST=1 declares "yes, this
#     host must survive an unattended reboot" and promotes those mismatches
#     to fail; left unset, they report as warn with an explanation. See
#     _spd_reboot_mismatch_sev/_spd_reboot_mismatch_note.
#
# Env var reference (all optional):
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST         "1" to treat this host as an
#                                            unattended remote-recovery target
#                                            (promotes Tier 1/2 mismatches to
#                                            fail instead of warn).
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_FILEVAULT    Expected `fdesetup status`
#                                            posture word (default: "Off",
#                                            the darren-m4-mini baseline).
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_AUTOLOGIN_USER  Expected autoLoginUser
#                                            (default: `id -un`).
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_ARD_USER            User to read ARD naprivs for
#                                            (default: `id -un`).
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_NAPRIVS      Expected naprivs mask substring
#                                            (default: "-1073741569", the ARD
#                                            full-access constant).
#   KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_TAILSCALE_BIN       Path to the Tailscale CLI binary
#                                            (default: the standard
#                                            /Applications/Tailscale.app
#                                            location).
#
# THIS SECTION IS READ-ONLY, same as every other check in this script: no
# `launchctl load`, no `pmset` writes, no service start/stop. Repair is
# scotty-sd's phase, not this subcommand's.

# _spd_reboot_mismatch_sev / _spd_reboot_mismatch_note
# The single lever that turns a Tier 1/2 "expected X, got Y" finding into
# either a real "fail" or an advisory "warn" — see the env var reference
# above. Both must be called together (sev decides the bucket, note explains
# why in the summary) so a reader never sees a bare "warn" with no
# indication that it could be a "fail" on a host where lockout truly matters.
_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev() {
    if [ "${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST:-0}" = "1" ]; then
        printf 'fail\n'
    else
        printf 'warn\n'
    fi
}
_spd_reboot_mismatch_note() {
    if [ "${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST:-0}" != "1" ]; then
        # XACA-1071-007: this clause fires on EVERY Tier 1/2 mismatch — up to 11
        # lines in one run. The full explanation lives once in the report header
        # (see _spd_reboot_report); repeating it per line adds scroll without
        # adding information after the first read, and this tool is read at 2am
        # on a broken box where scannability IS the feature. Short tag here,
        # full text there.
        printf ' [warn not fail — see NOTE above]'
    fi
}

# Shared helper for the four simple "is anything listening on this port"
# checks (trap #3 above: port-based, never process-name-based).
#   $1 surface  $2 port  $3 human label
_spd_reboot_check_port_listening() {
    local surface="$1" port="$2" label="$3"
    if ! _spd_have netstat; then
        _spd_emit "$surface" "unknown" "netstat not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$(netstat -an 2>/dev/null | grep -E "\\.${port} .*LISTEN")
    if [ -n "$out" ]; then
        _spd_emit "$surface" "ok" "$label listening on $port" "$out" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "$surface" "$sev" "$label not listening on $port$note" "" ""
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER 1 — REMOTE LOCKOUT RISK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_reboot_check_filevault() {
    if ! _spd_have fdesetup; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-filevault" "unknown" "fdesetup not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out expect
    out=$(fdesetup status 2>&1)
    expect="${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_FILEVAULT:-Off}"
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "FileVault is $expect"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-filevault" "ok" "FileVault is $expect (matches expected value)" "$out" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t1-filevault" "$sev" \
        "FileVault does not match expected '$expect' (got: $out).$note ASSUMED DEFAULT: '$expect' is the darren-m4-mini 2026-09-02 baseline, where FileVault-off is a deliberate, SETTLED exception — most hosts should expect 'On'. Override with KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_FILEVAULT to this host's actual intended posture." \
        "$out" ""
}

_spd_reboot_check_autologin() {
    if ! _spd_have defaults; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-autologin" "unknown" "'defaults' not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out rc expect
    out=$(defaults read /Library/Preferences/com.apple.loginwindow autoLoginUser 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    expect="${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_AUTOLOGIN_USER:-$(id -un)}"
    if [ $rc -eq 0 ] && [ "$out" = "$expect" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-autologin" "ok" "auto-login user set to '$expect'" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-autologin" "$sev" \
            "no auto-login user configured (expected '$expect', defaulted to the current interactive user via 'id -un' — override KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_AUTOLOGIN_USER if this host intends a different or no auto-login user at all).$note a headless reboot with no auto-login stalls at the login window." \
            "$out" "$rc"
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-autologin" "$sev" "auto-login user is '$out', expected '$expect'.$note" "$out" "$rc"
    fi
}

_spd_reboot_check_tailscale_sysext() {
    if ! _spd_have systemextensionsctl; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-sysext" "unknown" "systemextensionsctl not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$(systemextensionsctl list 2>&1 | grep -i tailscale)
    if [ -z "$out" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-sysext" "unknown" "no Tailscale system-extension entry found — Tailscale may not be installed on this host" "" ""
        return
    fi
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "activated enabled"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-sysext" "ok" "Tailscale sysext activated" "$out" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-sysext" "$sev" "Tailscale sysext not activated/enabled.$note" "$out" ""
}

_spd_reboot_check_tailscale_tunnel() {
    local bin="${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_TAILSCALE_BIN:-/Applications/Tailscale.app/Contents/MacOS/Tailscale}"
    if [ ! -x "$bin" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-tunnel" "unknown" "Tailscale binary not found at $bin — Tailscale may not be installed on this host (override KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_TAILSCALE_BIN if it lives elsewhere)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$("$bin" status 2>&1 | head -1)
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q '^100\.'; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-tunnel" "ok" "Tailscale tunnel up ($out)" "$out" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-tunnel" "$sev" "Tailscale tunnel not up (got: ${out:-<empty>}).$note" "$out" ""
}

_spd_reboot_check_sshd() {
    _spd_reboot_check_port_listening "reboot-t1-sshd" 22 "sshd (Remote Login)"
}

_spd_reboot_check_tailscale_login() {
    if ! _spd_have defaults; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-login" "unknown" "'defaults' not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out rc
    out=$(defaults read io.tailscale.ipn.macsys TailscaleStartOnLogin 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-login" "unknown" "TailscaleStartOnLogin key not set — Tailscale may not be installed via the macsys/standalone variant on this host" "$out" "$rc"
        return
    fi
    if [ "$out" = "1" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-login" "ok" "Tailscale starts on login" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t1-tailscale-login" "$sev" "Tailscale does not start on login (got: $out).$note" "$out" ""
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER 2 — REMOTE CONTROL / RECOVERY
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_reboot_check_ard_mode() {
    if ! _spd_have defaults; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-mode" "unknown" "'defaults' not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out rc
    out=$(defaults read /Library/Preferences/com.apple.RemoteManagement ARD_AllLocalUsers 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-mode" "unknown" "ARD_AllLocalUsers key not set — Remote Management may not be configured on this host" "$out" "$rc"
        return
    fi
    if [ "$out" = "0" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-mode" "ok" "ARD specified-users mode" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-mode" "$sev" "ARD is in all-local-users mode (got: $out), expected specified-users (0) per fleet ARD posture.$note" "$out" ""
}

_spd_reboot_check_ard_naprivs() {
    if ! _spd_have dscl; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-naprivs" "unknown" "'dscl' not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local user expect out rc
    user="${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_ARD_USER:-$(id -un)}"
    expect="${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_NAPRIVS:--1073741569}"
    out=$(dscl . -read "/Users/$user" naprivs 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-naprivs" "unknown" "naprivs attribute not readable for user '$user' (dscl exit $rc) — ARD may not have been granted to this user yet" "$out" "$rc"
        return
    fi
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q -- "$expect"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-naprivs" "ok" "ARD naprivs mask for '$user' matches expected value" "$out" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t2-ard-naprivs" "$sev" \
        "ARD naprivs mask for '$user' is '$out', expected to contain '$expect' (the ARD full-access constant, from the darren-m4-mini baseline — override KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_EXPECT_NAPRIVS if a narrower privilege set is intentional here).$note" \
        "$out" ""
}

_spd_reboot_check_screensharing() {
    _spd_reboot_check_port_listening "reboot-t2-screensharing" 5900 "Screen Sharing"
}

_spd_reboot_check_ard_agent() {
    _spd_reboot_check_port_listening "reboot-t2-ard-agent" 3283 "ARD agent"
}

_spd_reboot_check_autorestart() {
    if ! _spd_have pmset; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-autorestart" "unknown" "pmset not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    # Trap #1: word-anchor on $1 via awk — `grep autorestart` also matches
    # `autorestartatconnect` and a `head -1` on that output silently picks the
    # wrong line (cost a false failure on 2026-09-02).
    local raw val
    raw=$(pmset -g 2>&1)
    val=$(printf '%s\n' "$raw" | awk '$1=="autorestart"{print $2}')
    if [ -z "$val" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-autorestart" "unknown" "'autorestart' key not found in 'pmset -g' output (some Mac models/power sources don't expose it)" "$raw" ""
        return
    fi
    if [ "$val" = "1" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t2-autorestart" "ok" "autorestart after power failure enabled" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local sev note
    sev=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_sev)
    note=$(_spd_reboot_mismatch_note)
    _spd_emit "reboot-t2-autorestart" "$sev" "autorestart after power failure disabled (got: $val).$note" "$raw" ""
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER 3 — AITEAMFORGE STACK
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_reboot_check_homebrew() {
    if ! _spd_have brew; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-homebrew" "unknown" "brew not found on PATH" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$(brew --version 2>&1 | head -1)
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "^Homebrew"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-homebrew" "ok" "$out" "" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-homebrew" "unknown" "unexpected 'brew --version' output" "$out" ""
    fi
}

_spd_reboot_check_aiteamforge_cli() {
    if ! _spd_have aiteamforge; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-aiteamforge-cli" "unknown" \
            "aiteamforge CLI not found on PATH — expected on a tap-installed consumer host; CORRECTLY absent on the dev-source machine by hard policy (AITeamForge must never be installed alongside dev-team — see this repo's CLAUDE.md)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$(aiteamforge version 2>&1 | head -1)
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "AITeamForge"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-aiteamforge-cli" "ok" "$out" "" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-aiteamforge-cli" "unknown" "unexpected 'aiteamforge version' output" "$out" ""
    fi
}

_spd_reboot_check_clt() {
    if ! _spd_have xcode-select; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-clt" "unknown" "xcode-select not found (non-macOS host?)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out rc
    out=$(xcode-select -p 2>&1)
    rc=$?
    if [ $rc -ne 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-clt" "fail" "developer command-line tools not found ($out)" "$out" "$rc"
        return
    fi
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "CommandLineTools"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-clt" "ok" "Command Line Tools present ($out)" "" ""
    else
        # A full Xcode install (common on iOS/Android team hosts) also
        # satisfies "developer tools present" — CommandLineTools-only is the
        # M4Mini-style consumer baseline, not a universal requirement.
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-clt" "ok" "developer tools present via a full Xcode install ($out), not the CommandLineTools-only path" "$out" ""
    fi
}

_spd_reboot_check_python3() {
    if ! _spd_have python3; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-python3" "fail" "python3 not found on PATH" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local out
    out=$(python3 -V 2>&1)
    if printf '%s' "$out" | grep -q "^Python 3"; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-python3" "ok" "$out" "" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-python3" "unknown" "unexpected 'python3 -V' output" "$out" ""
    fi
}

# DE-HARDCODED: rather than assert a fixed list of "the mandatory 7"
# LaunchAgents (host-specific, and this script's charter says never invent a
# first-principles health judgement), this discovers whatever
# com.aiteamforge.*.plist files are actually INSTALLED on this host and
# checks only that each one is loaded. A host with zero such plists (e.g. the
# dev-source machine, where AITeamForge is never installed) is SKIP, not
# fail/unknown — that mirrors _spd_check_kb_sweep_stubs's dev-machine-only
# handling above.
_spd_reboot_check_launchagents() {
    local dir="$HOME/Library/LaunchAgents"
    if [ ! -d "$dir" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-launchagents" "unknown" "LaunchAgents directory not found: $dir" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local f label unloaded="" count=0
    for f in "$dir"/com.aiteamforge.*.plist; do
        [ -e "$f" ] || continue
        count=$((count + 1))
        label=$(basename "$f" .plist)
        if ! launchctl list "$label" >/dev/null 2>&1; then
            unloaded="$unloaded $label"
        fi
    done
    if [ "$count" -eq 0 ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-launchagents" "skip" \
            "no com.aiteamforge.* LaunchAgent plists installed on this host — expected where AITeamForge is not installed (e.g. the dev-source machine, by policy)" "" ""
        return
    fi
    if [ -n "$unloaded" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-launchagents" "fail" "installed but NOT loaded:$unloaded" "" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t3-launchagents" "ok" "$count com.aiteamforge.* LaunchAgent(s) installed and loaded" "" ""
    fi
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# TIER 4 — TEAMS
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DE-HARDCODED: team list is derived from ~/.aiteamforge/team-machines.json
# (every team whose declared machine normalizes to this host), not a
# hardcoded finance/legal/medical/freelance literal. "No sessions" is
# reported as warn (never fail) — same as the prototype's own DOWN/yellow
# bucket, annotated "expected — no auto-restore exists yet" — because an
# idle team after a reboot is the documented normal state, not a defect.
_spd_reboot_check_teams() {
    local map="$HOME/.aiteamforge/team-machines.json"
    if [ ! -f "$map" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t4-teams" "unknown" "team-machines.json not found at $map — cannot determine which teams belong to this host" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local host_norm teams
    host_norm=$(printf '%s' "$SPD_HOST" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]')
    teams=$(jq -r --arg h "$host_norm" \
        '. as $m | keys_unsorted[] | select(($m[.] | ascii_downcase | rtrimstr(".local")) == $h)' \
        "$map" 2>/dev/null)
    if [ -z "$teams" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t4-teams" "skip" "no teams declared for this host ('$SPD_HOST') in team-machines.json — nothing to verify" "" ""
        return
    fi
    local uid socket_dir team sessions n down="" up=0 total=0
    uid=$(id -u)
    socket_dir="/tmp/tmux-${uid}"
    while IFS= read -r team; do
        [ -z "$team" ] && continue
        total=$((total + 1))
        sessions=""
        if [ -S "$socket_dir/$team" ]; then
            sessions=$(tmux -S "$socket_dir/$team" list-sessions -F '#{session_name}' 2>/dev/null)
        fi
        if [ -z "$sessions" ]; then
            sessions=$(tmux -L "$team" list-sessions -F '#{session_name}' 2>/dev/null)
        fi
        n=$(printf '%s\n' "$sessions" | grep -c .)
        if [ "${n:-0}" -gt 0 ] 2>/dev/null; then
            up=$((up + 1))
        else
            down="$down $team"
        fi
    done <<SPD_REBOOT_TEAMS_EOF
$teams
SPD_REBOOT_TEAMS_EOF
    if [ -n "$down" ]; then
        _spd_emit "reboot-t4-teams" "warn" \
            "$up/$total team session(s) up for this host; down (expected — no auto-restore exists yet):$down" \
            "$teams" ""
    else
        _spd_emit "reboot-t4-teams" "ok" "all $up/$total team session(s) for this host are up" "$teams" ""
    fi
}

_spd_reboot_run_all_checks() {
    # Trap #2: tmux/brew/etc. live under /opt/homebrew/bin, not guaranteed on
    # PATH under launchd/cron/ssh invocation. Without this, tmux silently
    # reads as command-not-found and every Tier 4 team reports "down" on an
    # otherwise healthy host.
    export PATH="/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:$PATH"

    _spd_reboot_check_filevault
    _spd_reboot_check_autologin
    _spd_reboot_check_tailscale_sysext
    _spd_reboot_check_tailscale_tunnel
    _spd_reboot_check_sshd
    _spd_reboot_check_tailscale_login

    _spd_reboot_check_ard_mode
    _spd_reboot_check_ard_naprivs
    _spd_reboot_check_screensharing
    _spd_reboot_check_ard_agent
    _spd_reboot_check_autorestart

    _spd_reboot_check_homebrew
    _spd_reboot_check_aiteamforge_cli
    _spd_reboot_check_clt
    _spd_reboot_check_python3
    _spd_reboot_check_launchagents

    _spd_reboot_check_teams
}

# Tier-grouped report — same per-line format, severity vocabulary, and
# findings-file writer as _spd_report, just grouped by tier (via the
# "reboot-t<N>-*" surface prefix) INSTEAD of by severity, because tier order
# (lockout risk) is the point of this subcommand. Never files findings —
# `verify-reboot` is report-only, same posture as `report` (see CLI help).
_spd_reboot_report() {
    echo "==================================================================="
    echo "kb-spacedock verify-reboot — host: $SPD_HOST — $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "OS: $(sw_vers -productVersion 2>/dev/null || echo unknown) ($(sw_vers -buildVersion 2>/dev/null || echo unknown))"
    echo "Ordered by LOCKOUT RISK — resolve Tier 1 failures BEFORE relying on"
    echo "remote access or walking away from this machine."
    if [ "${KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST:-0}" != "1" ]; then
        echo
        echo "NOTE: KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST is not set to 1 — this host has not"
        echo "been declared a remote/unattended recovery target, so Tier 1/2 posture"
        echo "mismatches below are reported as 'warn' rather than 'fail'. Set it to 1"
        echo "on hosts like darren-m4-mini where lockout is a real operational risk."
    fi
    echo "==================================================================="

    local tier label f surface sev summary
    for tier in 1 2 3 4; do
        case "$tier" in
            1) label="TIER 1 -- REMOTE LOCKOUT RISK (fix before walking away)" ;;
            2) label="TIER 2 -- REMOTE CONTROL / RECOVERY" ;;
            3) label="TIER 3 -- AITEAMFORGE STACK" ;;
            4) label="TIER 4 -- TEAMS" ;;
        esac
        echo
        echo "-- $label --"
        for f in "${SPD_FINDINGS[@]+"${SPD_FINDINGS[@]}"}"; do
            surface=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.surface')
            case "$surface" in
                reboot-t${tier}-*) ;;
                *) continue ;;
            esac
            sev=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.severity')
            summary=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.summary')
            printf '  [%-7s] %-28s %s\n' "$sev" "$surface" "$summary"
        done
    done
    echo "-------------------------------------------------------------------"
    _spd_write_findings_file
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Reporting — prints a human summary, worst-first, and writes the full
# findings array to a JSON file. This file lives under $TMPDIR, deliberately
# NEVER under ~/.aiteamforge/spacedock/ — writing findings must never look
# like, or risk becoming, "creating the board".
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_worst_severity() {
    local s has_fail=0 has_unknown=0 has_warn=0 f
    for f in "${SPD_FINDINGS[@]+"${SPD_FINDINGS[@]}"}"; do
        s=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.severity')
        case "$s" in
            fail) has_fail=1 ;;
            unknown) has_unknown=1 ;;
            warn) has_warn=1 ;;
        esac
    done
    if [ "$has_fail" -eq 1 ] || [ "$has_unknown" -eq 1 ]; then
        printf '2\n'
    elif [ "$has_warn" -eq 1 ]; then
        printf '1\n'
    else
        printf '0\n'
    fi
}

#   $1 cmd  the subcommand that invoked this report (e.g. "triage", "report")
#           — shown verbatim in the header so `report`'s output never reads
#           as if `triage` (which files findings) had run. Defaults to
#           "triage" only as a defensive fallback if ever called without it.
_spd_report() {
    # PR #869 review: default to the NON-filing label. Both call sites pass this
    # explicitly today so the default is unreachable, but if one ever stops, a
    # header reading "triage" on a run that filed nothing is the more misleading
    # of the two errors.
    local cmd="${1:-report}"
    echo "==================================================================="
    echo "kb-spacedock $cmd — host: $SPD_HOST — $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)"
    echo "==================================================================="
    local order sev f surface summary any
    for order in fail unknown warn ok skip; do
        any=0
        for f in "${SPD_FINDINGS[@]+"${SPD_FINDINGS[@]}"}"; do
            sev=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.severity')
            [ "$sev" = "$order" ] || continue
            any=1
            surface=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.surface')
            summary=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.summary')
            printf '  [%-7s] %-22s %s\n' "$order" "$surface" "$summary"
        done
    done
    echo "-------------------------------------------------------------------"
    _spd_write_findings_file
}

# Shared tail of every report path: write the accumulated SPD_FINDINGS array to
# a JSON file under $TMPDIR (never under ~/.aiteamforge/spacedock/ — see the
# header note on why findings-writing must never look like board creation).
# Factored out so `report`/`triage` and `verify-reboot`'s tier-grouped report
# emit byte-identical findings-file JSON rather than two divergent writers.
_spd_write_findings_file() {
    mkdir -p "$SPD_FINDINGS_DIR" 2>/dev/null
    local ts findings_file
    ts=$(date -u +%Y%m%dT%H%M%SZ)
    findings_file="$SPD_FINDINGS_DIR/findings-${ts}.json"
    printf '%s\n' "${SPD_FINDINGS[@]+"${SPD_FINDINGS[@]}"}" | jq -s \
        --arg host "$SPD_HOST" --arg generated_at "$(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)" \
        '{host: $host, generated_at: $generated_at, findings: .}' > "$findings_file" 2>/dev/null
    echo "Findings written to: $findings_file"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Filing — the ONLY write path in this script. Gated entirely on the
# spacedock board file actually existing; never creates it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_file_findings() {
    if [ ! -f "$SPD_BOARD" ]; then
        echo ""
        echo "XSDK filing UNAVAILABLE — no spacedock board found at:"
        echo "  $SPD_BOARD"
        echo "The spacedock team/board does not exist on this host yet (XACA-1068 is"
        echo "provisioning it). Every diagnostic above still ran and was reported in full;"
        echo "nothing was filed. Do NOT create this board manually — re-run"
        echo "'kb-spacedock triage' once XACA-1068 has landed it on this host."
        return 0
    fi

    local kh
    kh=$(_spd_find_kanban_helpers) || {
        echo ""
        echo "XSDK filing UNAVAILABLE — a spacedock board exists, but no kanban-helpers.sh"
        echo "(or ported equivalent) could be located on this host to file through kb-backlog."
        return 0
    }
    local shell
    shell=$(_spd_kanban_helpers_shell "$kh")
    if ! _spd_have "$shell"; then
        echo ""
        echo "XSDK filing UNAVAILABLE — found kanban-helpers.sh but its interpreter ('$shell') is not on PATH."
        return 0
    fi

    echo ""
    echo "Filing warn/fail/unknown findings to the spacedock board..."
    local filed=0 f sev surface summary title priority out id
    for f in "${SPD_FINDINGS[@]+"${SPD_FINDINGS[@]}"}"; do
        sev=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.severity')
        case "$sev" in
            warn) priority="medium" ;;
            fail) priority="critical" ;;
            unknown) priority="high" ;;
            *) continue ;;
        esac
        surface=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.surface')
        summary=$(printf '%s' "$f" | jq -r '.summary')
        title="[$surface] $summary"

        out=$(env KB_TEAM=spacedock KB_TERMINAL=triage KB_SPD_KH="$kh" KB_SPD_TITLE="$title" KB_SPD_PRIORITY="$priority" \
            "$shell" -c 'source "$KB_SPD_KH" >/dev/null 2>&1 && kb-backlog add "$KB_SPD_TITLE" "$KB_SPD_PRIORITY"' 2>&1)

        id=$(printf '%s' "$out" | sed -nE 's/.*Added \[([A-Za-z0-9_-]+)\].*/\1/p' | head -1)
        if [ -n "$id" ]; then
            case "$id" in
                XSDK-*) ;;
                *) echo "  NOTE: minted id '$id' does not start with 'XSDK-' — possible series/prefix" \
                        "provisioning bug (tracked as an open question against XACA-1068), not this script's doing." ;;
            esac
            echo "  Filed: ${SPD_HOST}/${id}  —  $title"
            filed=$((filed + 1))
        else
            echo "  FAILED to file: $title"
            printf '%s\n' "$out" | sed 's/^/    /'
        fi
    done
    echo ""
    echo "$filed finding(s) filed. Cite them cross-machine as <host>/XSDK-nnnn (docs/spacedock-board-hygiene.md)."
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# CLI
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
_spd_usage() {
    cat <<'USAGE'
kb-spacedock — Space Dock host-recovery triage entrypoint

Usage:
  kb-spacedock triage    Run every diagnostic, report, write a findings file,
                          and file warn/fail/unknown findings as XSDK items
                          (only if a spacedock board already exists on this
                          host — never creates one).
  kb-spacedock report    Same diagnostics + report + findings file. Never
                          files anything, even if a board exists. Safe to
                          run repeatedly mid-incident.
  kb-spacedock verify-reboot
                          Post-reboot invariant check, ordered by LOCKOUT
                          RISK (Tier 1: remote lockout risk — FileVault,
                          autologin, Tailscale, sshd; Tier 2: remote
                          control/recovery — ARD, screen sharing,
                          autorestart; Tier 3: the AITeamForge stack; Tier 4:
                          per-team tmux sessions). Read-only; never files
                          anything, same as `report`. Productized from the
                          2026-09-02 darren-m4-mini reboot-survivability
                          harness (XACA-1071-013) — see the env var
                          reference in this script's REBOOT-SURVIVABILITY
                          CHECKS header comment for how to declare this host
                          as an unattended remote-recovery target
                          (KB_SPACEDOCK_REBOOT_REMOTE_HOST=1) and override
                          host-specific expected-value baselines
                          (FileVault/autologin/ARD naprivs).
  kb-spacedock help      This text.

Exit code reflects the WORST finding severity across all diagnostics run:
  0 = all ok/skip   1 = worst is warn   2 = worst is fail or unknown

This tool fronts existing diagnostics — it does not implement any health
judgement of its own beyond interpreting their documented exit codes/output.
See docs/spacedock-diagnostic-inventory.md for the full contract per surface.
USAGE
}

main() {
    local cmd="${1:-}"
    case "$cmd" in
        triage)
            _spd_run_all_checks
            _spd_report "$cmd"
            _spd_file_findings
            ;;
        report)
            _spd_run_all_checks
            _spd_report "$cmd"
            ;;
        verify-reboot)
            _spd_reboot_run_all_checks
            _spd_reboot_report
            ;;
        help|-h|--help|"")
            _spd_usage
            [ -z "$cmd" ] && return 2
            return 0
            ;;
        *)
            echo "kb-spacedock: unknown command '$cmd'" >&2
            _spd_usage >&2
            return 2
            ;;
    esac
    # NOTE: deliberately NOT `_spd_worst_severity | read code` — the last
    # stage of a pipeline runs in a subshell under default bash (no
    # `lastpipe`), so an `exit` there would only end the subshell and this
    # script would fall through with whatever exit code main() naturally
    # returns. Capture via command substitution instead, which runs in-shell.
    local code
    code=$(_spd_worst_severity)
    exit "$code"
}

main "$@"
